@startuml

title Work that outlives one token re-exchanges, and a worker node holds no token

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true
autonumber

participant "ores.workflow" as engine
participant "step handler" as handler
participant "run token cache" as cache
participant "ores.iam" as iam

== A step service whose work outlives a token ==

engine -> handler : step command
loop while the step's work continues
    handler -> cache : get (grant id, run id)
    alt a token with more than 20% of its life left
        cache --> handler : the run token
    else the first request, or close to expiry
        cache -> iam : iam.v1.run_grants.exchange
        iam --> cache : a signed token that lasts 5 minutes
        cache --> handler : the run token
    end
    handler -> handler : call an owner with the run token
end

note over handler, cache
  A run of four steps that lasts thirty minutes makes about seven exchanges,
  and a run of hours lives through many tokens. The grant id and the run id do
  not change, so the exchange spends one run of the grant's max_runs in total.
end note

== The engine's own work holds no run token ==

participant "worker node" as worker
participant "storage" as storage

engine -> worker : an assignment, when the tenant's share allows it
worker -> storage : read the engine package and the input (node identity)
worker -> worker : run the engine, which can take hours
worker -> storage : write the output archive (node identity)

note over worker
  The node holds no run token. An engine run can outlast any token, and a node
  serves many tenants, so the node acts under its own identity. That identity
  reaches only the keys the assignment names.
end note

@enduml
